        using var tx = _transactionFactory.BeginDeferredTransaction(_dbConnection);
        try
        {
            card.Update(newTitle, newDescription, newDueDate, DateTimeOffset.UtcNow);
            await _cardRepository.UpdateAsync(card, tx);  // <-- updates the card from boardB
            tx.Commit();
            return CardTransforms.ToDto(card);
        }
        catch { tx.Rollback(); throw; }
    });
}
```

CardRepository.FindByIdAsync SQL:
```sql
SELECT id, lane_id AS LaneId, board_id AS BoardId, title, description, due_date AS DueDate,
       position, version, created_at AS CreatedAt, updated_at AS UpdatedAt
FROM cards
WHERE id = @cardId   -- no board_id filter
```

CardEndpoints.cs (line 26-35):
```csharp
cards.MapPatch("cards/{cardId:guid}",
    async (Guid boardId, Guid cardId, UpdateCardRequest request, ICardService cardService) =>
        Results.Ok(await cardService.UpdateAsync(boardId, cardId, request)))
```

The route group is `/boards/{boardId:guid}` so `boardId` comes from the URL. `cardId` also comes from URL. There is NO check anywhere in the call chain that `card.BoardId == boardId`.

Also applies to DeleteAsync (same pattern) and MoveAsync (same pattern).

Is there any interceptor, middleware, database constraint, or other mechanism that would prevent this cross-board access? Note that the database schema has foreign key constraints but they enforce `lane_id → lanes.id` and `board_id → boards.id` independently — they do NOT enforce that `cards.board_id` matches `cards.lane_id`'s board. The UpdateAsync query only sets title/description/due_date — it does not change board_id or lane_id, so foreign keys don't trigger.

Return: CONFIRMED, PLAUSIBLE, or REFUTED with a one-sentence reason.

